&lt;h2&gt;&lt;strong&gt;About Buildkite&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/h2&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Buildkite&#39;s CI platform is trusted by the world&#39;s leading engineering teams, helping ship software used by more than 1 billion people every day. Companies including Canva, Meta, Mistral AI, and NVIDIA rely on Buildkite to run builds, tests, and deployments on their infrastructure, ours, or both — giving them the speed, reliability, and flexibility to ship with confidence.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;h2&gt;&lt;strong&gt;About the Role&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/h2&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Somewhere right now, an engineer has pushed a change and is watching a build run. What&#39;s doing that work is the Buildkite Agent — and that&#39;s what this team looks after. It’s the open-source agent that engineers around the world have running on their own machines, in their own clouds, in Buildkite’s own hosted agents, anywhere and everywhere Buildkite’s CI runs.&amp;nbsp;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;It&#39;s a critical repo to own. The team sits at an exciting intersection — deeply technical infrastructure work on one side, the fast-moving world of AI agents on the other, and our own agent sandwiched in the middle. It&#39;s the piece everything else leans on, so the details count. The people relying on you are your fellow engineers shipping their own features.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;The systems you&#39;ll look after can&#39;t be paused or casually restarted. We need to constantly iterate and update. That&#39;s the nature of it, and it&#39;s most of what makes it interesting. You&#39;ll have work you can point at and be proud of, and a sharp, opinionated team who&#39;ll happily spend an afternoon in a hard problem with you. A few months in, we&#39;d expect you to be fully owning something that matters.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;h2&gt;&lt;strong&gt;What You&#39;ll Do&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/h2&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Ship&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Break big problems into small things that go to production this week, rather than perfect things that go to production in eight months.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Own features end-to-end — from the idea, through the trade-offs, to the rollout, to what happens when it misbehaves in production.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Design for a world where things fail partially and constantly: careful rollouts, migrations you can reverse, and observability you&#39;d actually want during an incident.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Own&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Take a problem and get obsessive about it. Dig as deep as it takes. Come back with an answer and a plan.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Rotate through Sweep — two weeks in every eight, protecting the team&#39;s project time by fielding what comes in, working with Support and Solutions Engineers, and getting people answers fast. It&#39;s the quick-hit, high-endorphin end of the job.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Bring ideas to the roadmap, not just execution against it. If it&#39;s more valuable than what&#39;s already planned, we&#39;ll swap it in.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Handle incidents and put out problem embers before they become problematic fires.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Communicate&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Explain what you&#39;re doing, what you found, and what you&#39;re worried about — clearly, in writing and out loud, to engineers and non-engineers alike.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Pair, review, and give feedback that makes the code and the person better.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Use AI tooling like a power tool, not autopilot: throw an agent at the problem, then verify what comes back.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;
&lt;h2&gt;&lt;strong&gt;This Is You If...&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/h2&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;&lt;strong&gt;You&#39;d rather show us than tell us.&lt;/strong&gt; You can point at things you&#39;ve built, things you&#39;ve changed, and things you&#39;ve owned — and talk about them in the first person.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;&lt;strong&gt;You ship small and often.&lt;/strong&gt; Your instinct when facing an eight-month problem is to find the quick prototype and get it in front of people.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;&lt;strong&gt;You get obsessive.&lt;/strong&gt; When something&#39;s broken or slow or ugly, you can&#39;t leave it alone. You go find out why.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;&lt;strong&gt;You do it anyway.&lt;/strong&gt; You&#39;re comfortable saying &quot;I hadn&#39;t done this before and it was uncomfortable, but I worked out how&quot;. And &quot;that&#39;s not my job&quot; isn&#39;t in your vocabulary.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;&lt;strong&gt;You&#39;re a strong communicator.&lt;/strong&gt; You keep people around you informed without being asked. You&#39;re comfortable having an opinion in a room full of people who also have opinions.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;&lt;strong&gt;You have real production ownership behind you.&lt;/strong&gt; Not &quot;I was on the team&quot; but, &quot;I was responsible, and here&#39;s what broke and what I changed.&quot;&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;&lt;strong&gt;You are highly proficient with Go, or Ruby, and you&#39;re not precious about either.&lt;/strong&gt; Go is our primary language across the agent and infrastructure. Ruby on Rails is the backbone of the platform. You&#39;ll need genuine experience in at least one — and the comfort to move between languages as the tooling landscape shifts underneath all of us.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;
&lt;h2&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Bonus Points for experience with;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/h2&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Kubernetes &amp;amp; AWS&lt;/strong&gt; — we run cloud-native infrastructure at scale.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Terraform&lt;/strong&gt; — we manage infrastructure as code and value reusable, maintainable modules.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;&lt;strong&gt;PostgreSQL&lt;/strong&gt; — query optimisation, schema design, or running relational databases under load.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;&lt;strong&gt;React &amp;amp; GraphQL&lt;/strong&gt; — our frontend and API layer.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;You&#39;ve worked on developer tooling, CI/CD, or anything where your users are engineers with strong opinions.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;You&#39;ve written publicly about a problem you chased down — a post-mortem, a deep dive, a &quot;why is this slow&quot; investigation.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;
&lt;h2&gt;&lt;strong&gt;When You Apply — Come With a Story&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/h2&gt;
&lt;p&gt;This conversation will come up early, so maybe be prepared for it:&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Tell us about something you built and shipped quickly.&lt;/strong&gt; Not the biggest thing you&#39;ve ever worked on, but maybe the one where you broke a problem down, made a call, and got something small and useful into production faster than anyone expected. What did you skip? What did you get wrong? What happened next?&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;That conversation is the heart of our process. It&#39;s worth thinking about beforehand.&lt;/p&gt;
